    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter July 6 with a lineup anchored by the exceptional matchup of **Yordan Alvarez** (82.97), who benefits from a favorable 4.59 xERA against Mikolas and a robust xwOBA of 0.484, positioning him as our premier start. Joining him at the top of the production hierarchy are **Kyle Schwarber** (70.49) and **Trea Turner** (69.83); both capitalize on a favorable matchup against Cameron. **Trea Turner** remains our primary catalyst for stolen base efficiency, and despite an elite hold SP facing him, his lead-off role provides the necessary volume to justify his high floor. **Christian Yelich** (62.47) and **Carson Benge** (60.60) also present strong starts, with **Christian Yelich** seeing a significant +13% bump due to his platoon advantage against Dustin May, though we remain cautious regarding the 34% rain risk surrounding **Carson Benge**.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Lars Nootbaar** (37.76), who fell short of the 40-point threshold primarily due to a negative platoon split against Shane Drohan and a lower-tier batting order position. While his base projection remained respectable, the optimizer favored the higher ceilings and better situational advantages of **Nelson Velazquez** and the rest of our starting outfield. Beyond the active roster, we are monitoring the status of **Hyeseong Kim**, who remains in the minors with the Oklahoma City Comets, and we continue to look for opportunities to integrate depth pieces like **Colt Keith**, **Luke Raley**, **Dillon Dingler**, **Josh Smith**, **Chase Meidroth**, **Jacob Gonzalez**, **Luis Pena**, and **Pedro Ramirez** as matchups evolve. For today, we proceed with **Gary Sanchez**, **Kazuma Okamoto**, **Casey Schmitt**, and **Junior Caminero** in the remaining slots, accepting their marginal scores to preserve our daily caps while relying on our heavy hitters to carry the 5x5 categories.",
